\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Compact character expansions and surface integrals}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{G} be a compact Lie group with [normalized Haar
measure](fdip-007B). Choose unitary representatives of its complex
irreducibles, indexed by #{\widehat G}, with dimensions #{d_\rho}.
The Peter–Weyl theorem says that the functions
#{\sqrt{d_\rho}\rho(\cdot)_{ij}} form an orthonormal basis of
#{L^2(G)}. Its central subspace has orthonormal basis
#{\chi_\rho}. Thus a central #{f\in L^2(G)} has the expansion
##{f=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}c_\rho\chi_\rho,
 \qquad c_\rho=\int_G f(h)\overline{\chi_\rho(h)}\,d\mu_G(h),}
with convergence in #{L^2}. This is a \em{cited statement}, namely
Woit's Section 5, Theorem 1 and Corollary 1
\citet{p. 6}{woit2012peterweyl}. It does not assert pointwise or
absolute convergence for an arbitrary #{L^2} function.}

\p{For an inequivalent exhaustive family of finite-dimensional unitary
continuous models, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.peterWeylBasis} constructs the normalized
matrix-coefficient Hilbert basis, with its inner-product convention
for matrix coefficients. Over #{\mathbb C}, its coefficient
#{\langle\rho(g)e_a,e_b\rangle} is the conjugate of the matrix
entry #{\rho(g)_{ba}}; conjugation, or reindexing by dual
representations, gives the equivalent entry-coordinate basis.
The separate \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.characterBasis} constructs
the actual character basis of the central #{L^2} subspace.
Compact Hausdorff groups over #{\mathbb C}, hence the compact Lie
groups here, satisfy the stated hypotheses. These correspondences
support the two #{L^2} basis statements, not the uniform absolute
convergence or surface integral established next.}

\p{Suppose now that #{f} is continuous and central and that
##{\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}|c_\rho|d_\rho<\infty.}
Then its character expansion converges uniformly and absolutely.
For the [closed oriented polygonal surface](fdip-006V), with
#{P} faces and genus #{g}, define the edge integral
##{Z_\Sigma(f)=\int_{G^E}\prod_p f(U_p)\,d\mu(U).}
Under the displayed summability condition,
##{Z_\Sigma(f)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}
 d_\rho^{2-2g}\left(\frac{c_\rho}{d_\rho}\right)^P,}
and this numerical series is absolutely convergent.}

\proof{
\p{Unitarity gives #{|\chi_\rho(h)|\le d_\rho}. The sum of the
uniform bounds is finite, so the character series has a continuous
uniform limit. Peter–Weyl identifies that limit with #{f} in #{L^2};
continuity and full support of Haar measure make the equality
pointwise. Expanding a product over the finite face set gives a sum
whose total absolute value is bounded uniformly by
##{\left(\sum_\rho |c_\rho|d_\rho\right)^P.}
This bound is integrable against probability measure, justifying
interchange of the full face-label sum and the edge integrals.}

\p{Haar averaging gives the same matrix orthogonality as finite
averaging: for #{A:V_\tau\to V_\rho}, the integral
#{\int_G\rho(h)A\tau(h)^{-1}\,d\mu_G(h)} is an intertwiner.
Schur's lemma and its trace make it zero for inequivalent
representatives and #{(\operatorname{tr}A/d_\rho)I} for equal ones.
The edge contractions and circular vertex links in the
[finite surface proof](fdip-006W) therefore leave one common face
label and one free index per vertex. Their contribution is
#{c_\rho^P d_\rho^{|V|-|E|}}, the displayed formula.
Absolute convergence follows as well: the absolute values of these
integrated terms are bounded by the integrals of the absolute face
terms, whose total has just been bounded. In particular #{f=1}
still gives #{Z_\Sigma(1)=1}.}
}
